8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,8,8],[8,4,4,4,4,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,4,4,4,4,8,8],[8,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,8,8],[8,4,6,6,6,6,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,6,6,6,4,8,8],[8,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,8,8],[8,4,4,4,4,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,4,4,4,4,8,8],[8,8,1,1,1,1,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,1,1,1,1,8,8,8],[8,8,1,1,1,1,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,1,1,1,1,8,8,8],[8,8,1,1,1,1,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,1,1,1,1,8,8,8],[8,8,1,1,1,1,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,8,6,8,3,1,1,1,1,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the input contains.** Each input has two identical squares of one colour (colour 1 in every grid shown). One sits near the top‑left corner and the other lies further down and to the right. Only the top‑left square has decoration, and that decoration is a legend:\n\n- **To the right of the square** there are three columns: a *frame* colour, then background, then a *stripe* colour. In pair 1 these are 4, 0, 2. In pair 2 they are 2, 4(bg), 8.\n- **Below the square** there are three rows with the same layout: frame colour, background, stripe colour. In pair 1 these are 8, 0, 2. In pair 2 they are 3, 4(bg), 8.\n\n**What the output builds.** The two squares mark opposite corners of a rectangle.\n\n1. The square is copied to the two missing corners, so there is one square at each corner.\n2. **Horizontal bands** fill the gap between the left and right squares, in both the top and bottom square rows. Each band repeats the legend with period 4: frame, bg, stripe, bg, frame, … It starts in the column right after the left square and ends with a frame column touching the right square. This works because the gap width is always ≡ 1 (mod 4): 13 in pair 1, 13 in pair 2, 9 and 17 in the tests. A solid line of the horizontal frame colour closes each band on its outer side. For the top band that line is the row above the squares; for the bottom band it is the row below.\n3. **Vertical bands** do the same thing in the left and right square columns, using the legend read from below the square. The vertical gap is also ≡ 1 (mod 4): 13, 9, 13 and 17. A solid line of the vertical frame colour closes each band on its outer side: the column left of the left squares and the column right of the right squares.\n\nI checked this description against both demonstrations. In pair 1, row 1 and row 21 are solid 4 from column 6 to column 18. Column 2 and column 22 are solid 8 from row 5 to row 17. The columns inside the top band read 4 0 2 0 4 0 2 0 4 0 2 0 4. Pair 2 matches in the same way, with the frame line sitting at the grid border (column 0).\n\n**Applying it to the tests.**\n- Test 1 has 1×1 squares at (2,2) and (16,12). The horizontal legend is 2, 0, 8 and the vertical legend is 4, 0, 8. Columns 3–11 get the pattern 2 0 8 0 2 0 8 0 2, with solid 2 lines in rows 1 and 17. Rows 3–15 get the pattern 4 0 8 0 … 4, with solid 4 lines in columns 1 and 13.\n- Test 2 has 4×4 squares at (1,2) and (22,23). The horizontal legend is 3, 8, 6 and the vertical legend is 4, 8, 6. The gap is 17 cells in both directions.\n\n**How the program finds things.** The square colour is the colour of the top‑left non‑background cell. Square A is the component of that colour containing that cell. Square B is the other component of the same colour. Both legends are read from the cells next to A, and a background cell is appended to make the 4‑cell period.\n\nThe program emits three stages: the corner squares, then the horizontal bands, then the vertical bands.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    ys, xs = np.nonzero(g != bg)\n    order = sorted(zip(ys, xs), key=lambda p: (p[0] + p[1], p[0]))\n    sy, sx = order[0]\n    col = g[sy, sx]\n    comps = components(g == col)\n    A = [c for c in comps if (sy, sx) in c][0]\n    others = [c for c in comps if (sy, sx) not in c]\n    B = max(others, key=lambda c: bbox(c)[0] + bbox(c)[1])\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(A)\n    by0, bx0, by1, bx1 = bbox(B)\n    h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n\n    # legends\n    hseq = [g[y0, x1 + 1 + k] for k in range(3)] + [bg]\n    vseq = [g[y1 + 1 + k, x0] for k in range(3)] + [bg]\n\n    # 1. squares at all four corners\n    sq = g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1].copy()\n    for (ty, tx) in [(y0, x0), (y0, bx0), (by0, x0), (by0, bx0)]:\n        out[ty:ty + h, tx:tx + w] = sq\n    emit(out, \"Copy the square to all four corners of the rectangle defined by the two squares.\")\n\n    # 2. horizontal bands (top and bottom rows of squares)\n    for x in range(x1 + 1, bx0):\n        c = hseq[(x - (x1 + 1)) % 4]\n        out[y0:y1 + 1, x] = c\n        out[by0:by1 + 1, x] = c\n        if y0 - 1 >= 0:\n            out[y0 - 1, x] = hseq[0]\n        if by1 + 1 < H:\n            out[by1 + 1, x] = hseq[0]\n    emit(out, \"Fill horizontal gaps with the repeating legend (frame, bg, stripe, bg) and close them with an outer frame line.\")\n\n    # 3. vertical bands (left and right columns of squares)\n    for y in range(y1 + 1, by0):\n        c = vseq[(y - (y1 + 1)) % 4]\n        out[y, x0:x1 + 1] = c\n        out[y, bx0:bx1 + 1] = c\n        if x0 - 1 >= 0:\n            out[y, x0 - 1] = vseq[0]\n        if bx1 + 1 < W:\n            out[y, bx1 + 1] = vseq[0]\n    emit(out, \"Fill vertical gaps with the vertical legend and close them with an outer frame line.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Only the top‑left square has decoration, and that decoration is a legend:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each band repeats 